  "query_preview": "..Wednesday, a closed low south of the Aleutian chain\nwill reach full maturity, beginning to decay as it lifts north. \nThree primary features of interest as this system moves into the \nGulf are: strength of the accompanying surface low, clear/warm \nover the Panhandle for the mid to end of the week, and moisture \ntransport into the region by the weekend. Touching on the low \nfirst, ensembles show agreement that this system will make \nlandfall along the AK Peninsula, with southerlies building in the \ncentral Gulf; winds remain below gale force. Our stubborn high \nover the eastern Gulf cedes territory, making a slight retreat \ninto the Panhandle, with north-westerlies still impacting much of \nthe coastal waters. Overall, little concern for major marine \nimpacts with continued focus for northwest winds of moderate to \nfresh breezes along the Alexander Archipelago and sea breezes thru\nthe inside.\n\nAs this high shifts into the Panhandle Thursday, warm and dry \nonce again becomes the theme. Digging into the 850mb temps, the \nLREF shows a left-skewed distribution based around 7C to 15C, with\nthe mode around 13C, translating to 75F to 85F surface temps, \nabout 10F above normal for this time of year. The Extreme Forecast\nIndex shines more light into this, with agreement in abnormal \nwarmth increasing its footprint over the Panhandle into the end of\nthis week. A note, it\u2019s not just about the afternoon high \ntemperatures, nightly low temperatures also likely remain about \n10F above normal for most of the region. \n\nMoving onto moisture, by the end of the weekend this pattern will\nbegin to break down the dominant high, or at a minimum, push the \nridge further east, with southwesterlies picking up on some \nequatorial moisture and funneling it into the Gulf. A few ensemble\noutliers want to bring a quick hit of moderate AR conditions; \nhowever, overall modest Integrated Vapor Transport values suggest \njust an increase in clouds, and perhaps some light rain, by the \nend of the weekend. \n\nSimilar to previous days, winds of sustained 10 to 15 kts will\ncontinue through this afternoon, with the highest chance of seeing\nany gusts reaching 20 kts being in the vicinity of Taiya Inlet. \nNo LLWS concerns through the TAF period.",
